GAL22V10
816DEC02

CLK2 RWB  A12  A13  A14    A15   A16   A17   A18    A23    NC   GND
NC   LE   NC   NC   VRAMCE RAMCE ROMCE IOSEL OEGATE WEGATE PHI2 VCC

PHI2.R = /PHI2

LE = /PHI2 * /CLK2

/ROMCE = /A23 * /A18 * /A17 * /A16 * A15

/RAMCE = /A23 * /A18 * /A17 * /A16 * /A15 * /A14 + /A23 * /A18 * /A17 * /A16 * /A15 * A14 * /A13 + /A23 * A16 + /A23 * A17 + /A23 * A18

/VRAMCE = A23

/IOSEL = /A23 * /A18 * /A17 * /A16 * /A15 * A14 * A13

/OEGATE = PHI2 * RWB

/WEGATE = PHI2 * /RWB

AR = GND

SP = GND

DESCRIPTION

W65C816S address decode, clock divider and bank latch enable.
Target: ATF22V10C-7PX.

Supersedes 816DEC01. Changes: PHI2 is now generated here as a divide-by-two
of a 2x oscillator on pin 1, and LE for the bank latch is generated here as
well. See NOTE 6.


PIN ASSIGNMENT

 1  CLK2    oscillator at 2x the intended PHI2 frequency
 2  RWB     CPU pin 34
 3  A12     CPU pin 22 (declared, not used -- see NOTE 4)
 4  A13     CPU pin 23
 5  A14     CPU pin 24
 6  A15     CPU pin 25
 7  A16     74xx573 Q0
 8  A17     74xx573 Q1
 9  A18     74xx573 Q2
10  A23     74xx573 Q7
11  NC
12  GND
13  NC
14  LE      74xx573 pin 11 LE
15  NC      spare macrocell
16  NC      spare macrocell
17  VRAMCE  62256 pin 20  /CE
18  RAMCE   AS6C4008 pin 22 /CE
19  ROMCE   AT28C256 pin 20 /CE
20  IOSEL   74HC138 pin 4  /E1
21  OEGATE  AS6C4008 /OE, 62256 /OE, AT28C256 /OE
22  WEGATE  AS6C4008 /WE, 62256 /WE
23  PHI2    CPU pin 37, W65C22 pin 25, W65C51N pin 27

The 74xx573 /OE (pin 1) is tied to GND. Everything that previously took the
clock from the oscillator now takes it from pin 23.


MEMORY MAP

$00:0000-$00:5FFF   RAM   AS6C4008
$00:6000-$00:7FFF   I/O   74HC138, eight 1 KB slots on A10-A12
$00:8000-$00:FFFF   ROM   AT28C256
$01:0000-$07:FFFF   RAM   AS6C4008
$80:0000-$80:7FFF   VRAM  62256

A19-A22 are not decoded. Banks $08-$7F alias banks $00-$07; banks
$81-$FF alias bank $80. Within bank $80 the 62256 aliases every 32 KB.
See NOTE 3.


NOTE 1 -- SHARED OUTPUT ENABLE AND WRITE ENABLE

OEGATE and WEGATE are common to all three memory devices. This is safe
because ROMCE, RAMCE and VRAMCE are mutually exclusive, so at most one
device has its outputs enabled at any time.

OEGATE and WEGATE are also mutually exclusive by construction, since one
requires RWB high and the other RWB low. The SRAMs never see /OE and /WE
low simultaneously.

WEGATE includes PHI2 so that no write can occur while PHI2 is low. Raw
RWB would allow a write during PHI2 low, when D0-D7 still carries the
bank byte rather than write data.


NOTE 2 -- CHIP SELECTS ARE NOT PHI2 GATED

ROMCE, RAMCE, VRAMCE and IOSEL depend on A16-A23, which come from the
74xx573. That latch is transparent during the second quarter of each cycle
(see NOTE 6), so those inputs are briefly unsettled there and the selects
can glitch during that window.

The glitches are harmless. Writes are blocked because WEGATE requires PHI2
high, and the latch is closed for the whole of PHI2 high. Reads latch
nothing -- the CPU samples D0-D7 at the falling edge of PHI2, by which point
the bank byte has long since settled. The 65C22 and 65C51 likewise sample CS
and RS at the falling edge of PHI2.

Leaving the selects ungated gives ROM and RAM the longest possible access
window, which matters at higher clock speeds.

If a PHI2 qualifier is wanted later, the 74HC138 has /E2 and E3 free. Tie
/E2 to /PHI2 -- no reburn required.


NOTE 3 -- UNDECODED ADDRESS LINES

A19-A22 are deliberately left out of the array. Nothing is populated above
bank $07 other than video, so the aliasing costs nothing today.

Tighten this first if a second RAM device or a second video region is ever
added. Two spare macrocells and three free input pins (11, 13, and one spare
reconfigured as an input) are available for that.


NOTE 4 -- A12

A12 is wired to the GAL but unused. The I/O window is split externally by
the 74HC138 on A10-A12, so moving the VIA or ACIA between slots is a wire
change on the decoder rather than a reburn.

A12 is present so that finer I/O decode can be pulled into the GAL later
without adding a wire. If Galette rejects an unused input pin, change pin 3
to NC and leave the trace unconnected.


NOTE 5 -- ATF22V10C POWER-DOWN

The power-down feature is enabled at the silicon level on this part and is
not controlled from this file. Disable it at programming time by writing
row 59, then verify pin 4 behaviour on the bench after the first burn.


NOTE 6 -- CLOCK DIVIDER AND LATCH ENABLE

Pin 1 takes an oscillator at twice the intended PHI2 frequency. PHI2 is a
registered output that toggles on every rising edge of CLK2, so PHI2 has a
50% duty cycle at half the oscillator frequency.

LE is high only during the second quarter of each bus cycle:

  0     PHI2 falls, new cycle starts; LE low, latch holds previous bank
  T/4   CLK2 falls, LE rises, latch transparent, new bank bits pass through
  T/2   CLK2 rises, PHI2 rises and LE falls on the same edge, latch closed
  T     PHI2 falls

This bounds both edges of LE, which a plain inverted PHI2 cannot do.

Closing edge. LE falls at CLK2 rising plus the GAL's combinational delay
(7.5 ns max). The latch needs the bank byte for its hold time after that
(2 ns for a 74ACT573). PHI2 rises at the same CLK2 edge plus the registered
clock-to-output delay, and the CPU guarantees the bank byte for 10 ns past
that. The margin is the clock-to-output delay plus 0.5 ns, and it holds
whatever that delay turns out to be, because both signals come off the same
edge of the same device.

Opening edge. LE rises a quarter cycle after PHI2 falls. WEGATE has long
since released (7.5 ns max after PHI2 falls) and the CPU's read data hold
time (10 ns) has passed, so the latch cannot disturb A16-A23 while an access
is still in progress. This requires T/4 to exceed roughly 9 ns, which is
satisfied at any clock this design can run.

Speed cost. The latch opens at T/4 rather than immediately, but the bank
byte does not arrive until 33 ns after PHI2 falls in any case. For cycles
longer than 132 ns the late opening costs nothing at all. The binding read
constraint is tACE: bank valid at RAM 44 ns, RAMCE 51.5 ns, plus 55 ns
access and 10 ns CPU setup gives a minimum cycle of 116.5 ns, about 8.5 MHz.

Single-stepping. Each manual clock pulse is now half a bus cycle. Two pulses
advance the CPU by one cycle.


NOTE 7 -- ITEMS TO VERIFY BEFORE RELYING ON THIS FILE

a) Pin 1 is used both as the register clock and as a logic term in LE.
   The GAL22V10 architecture allows this, but confirm Galette accepts it.
   If it does not, divide externally with a 74AC74, feed CLK2 on pin 1 and
   PHI2 on pin 11 as plain inputs, delete the PHI2, AR and SP equations,
   and keep LE combinational.

b) AR and SP syntax. GALasm requires asynchronous reset and synchronous
   preset terms for designs with registered outputs. Confirm the exact
   form Galette expects against the GALasm documentation before burning.

c) Power-up phase. The 22V10's registers power up reset, so check on the
   bench which phase PHI2 starts in and that reset behaves.

d) Output level. PHI2 now drives the CPU clock input from a GAL output.
   The ATF22V10C guarantees VOH of only 2.4 V at 4 mA, while the W65C816S
   needs 3.5 V. Measure it.
